title Fold AC -- the appeal cluster, and the reading that bought five folds
prompt Keaton set the loop running: pick up the running thread, take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, Standfast on reds first, send each finished increment as its own signed round.
think Standfast first. Every REDS row reading as open by a bare grep is an artifact of a closed-range fold -- the ledger stands at 110 rows, none open, and the newest pair (%110 and %110 CLOSED) both stand at 20260821.073606. No red preempts constructive work this lap.
think Council rota, lap index 591 kyri logs mod 27 -> item 24, active-designing/20260702-184312_the-grain-and-the-crossing.md. Deep-read. Its sameness strand is this arc's own law written before the arc began: one value model, one fold, one vocabulary, and count the whole cost of a line saved. A fold is that strand applied to a ladder.
think The running thread from fold AB named the crux by measurement: read_the_appeal at 27 rungs by 56 lines, 1456 carried. The carry scan confirmed it on metal before anything moved.
think Hashing every read_the_appeal on the ladder found twenty-eight bodies at one length -- twenty-seven byte for byte alike, and appeal.rye apart. Diffing the odd one showed all eight differing lines differ the same way: the owner reaches its own report directly where every rung above reaches through appeal_of. The tier owner stays home, exactly as amend.rye did in fold AA.
think Fold Z's rule ran before scope was chosen. read_the_appeal reaches thirteen symbols; twelve already stood public in all twenty-seven, and appeal_of cost one word in nineteen. That alone was a clean lap.
think Reading the closure further changed the lap. Four of those twelve are themselves whole carrying families -- address_published in 35 rungs, told_of in 29, pressed_by in 28, appeal_published in 28, each byte for byte alike at 13, 13, 13 and 12 lines. Each is a wire reader that opens a note through its own rung's path builder and plan name, so option B carries them exactly as it carries a check.
think The fold line was checked before scope was fixed. None of the four cohorts touches a module ladder_checks.rye imports -- cohort, cycle, confer, entrust, gap and the rest -- so all five families stand above the harness and none would invert the ladder.
think appeal_note stayed home on its own merit: a three-line body traded for a three-line delegate moves nothing, and the queue is right to leave it.
think Wrote the five bodies into caravan/ladder_checks.rye reaching everything through the rung handed in, widened note_path in 35, tell_path in 29, press_path in 28 and appeal_of in 19 -- 111 words, no accessor born -- then replaced each body with a delegate and repinned the meter, its ceiling, and the queue prose.
obs A first replacement pass dropped the signature lines rather than the bodies. The awk regex escaping failed loudly before that, so nothing had been written when the shape was wrong; the second attempt was caught by reading one delegate rather than by a compiler, reverted with git checkout, and redone keeping the signature. Measurement before the claim, as always.
obs The arithmetic seemed to leave a remainder and did not. Out went 26x56, 34x13, 28x13, 27x13 and 27x12 at 2,937; back came the same five cohorts at three lines apiece at 426; the fall of 2,511 stood against a meter reading 2,537.
obs The extra twenty-six was a fifth gift rather than a discrepancy. Widening note_path split a forty-five-rung family into thirty-five public and ten private, and a family split into two smaller ones carries one fewer copy of itself -- 44x26 becoming 34x26 plus 9x26. That is also why carrying families rose 706 -> 707. Fold Z's split-by-the-word-pub recurred a third time and this time paid.
obs The lesson is that the reach closure has depth. Fold AB read one hop out and found a cluster; this lap read one hop out and found four families larger than several the queue had been printing all along. A body's own reach is the cheapest survey on the ladder, since every symbol it names is already audited by the fold that lifts it.
